WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.397
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:09.129
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:15.960
Unit fourteen: Game Theory. Today you will describe strategic reasoning using modeling language.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.040
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.186
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.190
Game theory.

7
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:30.830
Game theory means the study of strategic decisions where each choice depends on others'.

8
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:34.937
Game theory is used in economics, politics and biology.

9
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.318
Your turn. Say: Game theory.

10
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.319
Game theory.

11
00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:44.584
Nash equilibrium.

12
00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:51.066
Nash equilibrium means a situation where no player gains by changing strategy alone.

13
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.776
At a Nash equilibrium, nobody wants to deviate.

14
00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:58.175
Your turn. Say: Nash equilibrium.

15
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.801
Nash equilibrium.

16
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.899
Zero-sum game.

17
00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:10.971
Zero-sum game means a situation in which one side's gain is exactly the other's loss.

18
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:14.872
Chess is a zero-sum game; trade need not be.

19
00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:18.403
Your turn. Say: Zero-sum game.

20
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:22.790
Zero-sum game.

21
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.934
Payoff matrix.

22
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.376
Payoff matrix means a table showing the outcomes of each combination of choices.

23
00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:34.121
The payoff matrix revealed why both firms cut prices.

24
00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:37.950
Your turn. Say: Payoff matrix.

25
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.138
Payoff matrix.

26
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:43.873
Strategy.

27
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:48.369
Strategy means a plan of action designed to achieve a goal.

28
00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:52.260
A good strategy anticipates the other side's response.

29
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.433
Your turn. Say: Strategy.

30
00:01:58.267 --> 00:01:59.488
Strategy.

31
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.530
Rational actor.

32
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:07.419
Rational actor means a decision-maker who pursues goals consistently and logically.

33
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:11.758
Models often assume a rational actor, though real people are messier.

34
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.316
Your turn. Say: Rational actor.

35
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.493
Rational actor.

36
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.425
Cooperation.

37
00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:25.577
Cooperation means working together toward a shared aim.

38
00:02:26.183 --> 00:02:30.052
Cooperation can emerge even among self-interested players.

39
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.395
Your turn. Say: Cooperation.

40
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:37.501
Cooperation.

41
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.228
Defection.

42
00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:44.352
Defection means choosing self-interest and breaking an agreement to cooperate.

43
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.669
One player's defection destroyed the cartel.

44
00:02:48.283 --> 00:02:50.796
Your turn. Say: Defection.

45
00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:54.565
Defection.

46
00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:59.377
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:04.863
Visal, why do two rival shops often cut prices until both lose money?

48
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:14.802
Game theory models how rational actors make strategic choices. Each shop fears that the other will defect first.

49
00:03:15.917 --> 00:03:18.613
But both would profit if they kept prices high.

50
00:03:19.717 --> 00:03:28.647
True, and a payoff matrix represents that tension: cooperation pays best collectively, yet defection tempts each player individually.

51
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.250
So is it hopeless?

52
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:41.843
No. Repeated interaction simulates trust over time. Models show that cooperation can emerge when players expect to meet again.

53
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:50.322
Grammar focus: Modeling language (models, represents, simulates).

55
00:03:50.622 --> 00:03:51.867
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.583 --> 00:03:56.313
Game theory models how rational actors make strategic choices.

57
00:03:57.217 --> 00:04:01.527
A payoff matrix represents the outcomes of every combination of choices.

58
00:04:02.417 --> 00:04:05.055
Repeated play simulates the growth of trust.

59
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:14.966
Verbs of modelling (model, represent, simulate, capture, illustrate) are followed by an object or a wh-clause: models how…, illustrates why…. Not 'models that how'.

60
00:04:15.717 --> 00:04:20.459
Be careful! Don't say: Game theory models as rational actors choose.

61
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.002
Say: Game theory models how rational actors choose.

62
00:04:26.302 --> 00:04:28.116
models + how / what clause.

63
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.518
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:34.031
The Logic of Strategy.

65
00:04:34.331 --> 00:04:40.127
Game theory models situations in which the outcome for each participant depends on the choices of the others.

66
00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:49.356
In a zero-sum game, one player's gain is exactly the other's loss, but many real interactions, such as trade, allow mutual benefit.

67
00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:58.612
A Nash equilibrium represents a stable outcome in which no player could improve by changing strategy alone, even if the result is collectively poor.

68
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:07.521
The prisoner's dilemma illustrates this: each player's defection is individually rational, yet mutual defection leaves both worse off.

69
00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:15.711
When interactions are repeated, however, cooperation can become the rational choice, since trust offers lasting rewards.

70
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.790
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.089
Game theory blank how actors make strategic choices.

72
00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:29.112
A, models. B, model to. C, modelling of.

73
00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:34.388
The answer is A: models.

74
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.036
A table of outcomes for each combination of choices is a blank.

75
00:05:39.336 --> 00:05:43.303
A, strategy. B, payoff matrix. C, defection.

76
00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:49.516
The answer is B: payoff matrix.

77
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.240
A situation where one side's gain is the other's loss is a blank.

78
00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:59.796
A, zero-sum game. B, Nash equilibrium. C, cooperation.

79
00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:05.797
The answer is A: zero-sum game.

80
00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.729
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:09.029 --> 00:06:17.918
A, The model illustrates why trust matters.. B, The model illustrates that why trust matters.. C, The model illustrates for trust matter..

82
00:06:21.467 --> 00:06:25.138
The answer is A: The model illustrates why trust matters..

83
00:06:26.283 --> 00:06:29.792
According to the reading, repeated interactions can make…

84
00:06:30.092 --> 00:06:36.253
A, defection always rational. B, cooperation rational. C, strategy irrelevant.

85
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.554
The answer is B: cooperation rational.

86
00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:46.953
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:47.817 --> 00:06:53.578
What is an example of a real-life situation that resembles a game theory scenario?

88
00:07:10.383 --> 00:07:11.907
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:12.207 --> 00:07:40.676
Two neighbouring farmers sharing one irrigation canal face a game theory scenario. If both use only their fair share, the canal lasts and both benefit. If one takes extra water, he gains in the short run while harming the other. Each fears the other's defection, so overuse can become an equilibrium. Rules and repeated relationships help sustain cooperation.

90
00:07:40.976 --> 00:07:42.662
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:49.801
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modeling language (models, represents, simulates).

92
00:07:50.101 --> 00:07:55.633
Today's value is strategic reasoning. Think ahead about how others will respond to your choices.

93
00:07:55.933 --> 00:07:58.609
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:01.770
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

